# synth.hal — write a song in plain words, get a .wav file.
# Run it:  hal -d examples/synth.hal [output-name]
# It writes <output-name>.wav (default song.wav) into the folder you run it from.

The song:
    tempo 92 bpm, key of A minor, 16 bars of 4/4
    chords, one per bar, repeating: Am  F  C  G
    bars 13 to 16 are the ending: Am  F  G  Am, and the last bar rings out

The instruments, all synthesized from scratch, no samples:
    pad    soft and wide, holds each chord, slow attack and release,
           slightly detuned and spread across left and right
    bass   plucky, plays the root of each chord on beats 1 and 3, with a
           short slide up into the next bar's root
    lead   a gentle bell-like tone, enters at bar 5, plays a simple melody
           you write that only uses notes from the current chord or the
           A minor scale, and ends on A
    drums  kick on 1 and 3, snare on 2 and 4, closed hats on eighth notes
           with a little swing. Drums start at bar 5 and stop for the
           last bar.

Mix it so nothing clips: leave about 1 dB of headroom, keep the bass
centered, and add a light reverb on the pad and lead. Fade in over the
first half-second and fade out over the last two seconds.

Write a 44.1 kHz, 16-bit, stereo WAV.

While rendering, draw a piano roll in the terminal that scrolls as each
bar is finished: one row per pitch that is used, a colored block per note
(a different color for each instrument), bar numbers along the bottom.
Use 24-bit color, because stdout is not a terminal. When done, print the
file's full path, its length in seconds, and its size.

The same input must always give exactly the same file. If anything needs
randomness, seed it with a fixed number.
